---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../utils/paths';
---

<BaseLayout title="About - Yonatan N. Degefu" description="Why Yonatan N. Degefu approaches biology through computation, and the questions about treatment resistance, biomarkers, and digital twins that motivate the next stage of the work.">
  <div class="about-container">
    <p class="eyebrow page-kicker">What motivates me</p>
    <h1>About Me</h1>

    <section class="bio-section">
      <p class="about-lede">
        I think of myself as a biologist first. The questions that draw me in are biological ones:
        why a treatment works for one patient but not another, and why a tumor that responds at first
        can stop responding. Computation is the tool I use to answer them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Living systems have too many interacting parts to reason about by intuition alone, so I use
        models and data to make ideas precise enough to test. I pay closest attention when a model
        and an experiment disagree, because that’s often where there is something new to learn.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bio-section" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <h2 id="future-heading">Questions I want to pursue next</h2>
      <p>
        I’m drawn to problems that span discovery and translation, where understanding how a disease
        works leads to a better way to choose or design a treatment.
      </p>

      <h3>Why do some cancer cells survive treatment that kills their neighbors?</h3>
      <p>
        Tumor cells with the same genome can take on different states, and some of those states
        tolerate treatment long enough to drive relapse. I want to understand how cells make these
        decisions, using large single-cell and multi-omic datasets and interpretable machine learning
        to uncover non-genetic sources of resistance. The patterns that signal a cell is about to
        escape could explain how resistance begins, and could become biomarkers that identify which
        patients need a different treatment strategy.
      </p>

      <h3>Can we simulate a treatment before it reaches patients?</h3>
      <p>
        Longer term, I want to build digital twin models: mechanistic models, calibrated to measured
        data, that simulate how cells respond when conditions change, such as when a drug is added or
        a signal is blocked. Each new measurement would refine the model, and each simulation would
        suggest the next experiment. Scaled up to virtual patient populations, models like these could
        help compare drug combinations and doses across the range of patients a therapy will meet in
        the clinic.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bio-section" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
      <h2 id="why-heading">Why it matters</h2>
      <p>
        What matters in the end is the patient. Treatments often stop working because cells adapt,
        sometimes without any genetic change. Understanding and simulating those decisions is how I
        hope to help design therapies that anticipate that adaptation instead of reacting to it, in
        cancer and other diseases.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the projects and methods I’ve worked on so far, see my
        <a href={withBase('/research/')}>research</a> and <a href={withBase('/cv/')}>CV</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bio-section" aria-labelledby="beyond-heading">
      <h2 id="beyond-heading">Beyond research</h2>
      <p>
        I enjoy spending time outdoors and cooking, and I speak English and Amharic. I also care about
        mentoring, community service, and making science accessible beyond the lab.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
